<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s1">
  <p class="st-k rv">01 · Challenge</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s1">A rotating timetable, built by hand</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    Keystone runs an 8-day rotating schedule. Every cycle has to line up teachers, student groups,
    classrooms and time slots, and the timetable was built by hand in spreadsheets. That caused four
    problems.
  </p>
  <div class="st-grid st-g4 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Problem 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Workload mismatches</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Teachers ended up over or under their maximum load.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Problem 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Idle or overfull rooms</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Venues were not matched to what each course needed.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Problem 03</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Inconsistent assignments</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Subjects were not always taught by qualified staff.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Problem 04</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Little transparency</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Staff could not see how decisions were made.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    As the school grows, so does the work, and administrators, teachers and planners would all use
    the new tool.
  </p>
  <div class="st-callout rv">
    <p class="st-bh">
      How can an optimizer’s output stay understandable and adjustable, so an administrator can see
      why a schedule looks the way it does, change the rules, and try alternatives without losing
      earlier work?
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s2">
  <p class="st-k rv">02 · Process</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s2">Sprints, approvals and many UI rounds</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    The plan was 15 weeks of two-week sprints. Each design went to Keystone for approval, with up to
    two rounds of feedback per wireframe.
  </p>
  <h3 class="st-sub rv">The planned sprints</h3>
  <ol class="st-list st-wide rv">
    <li>Scope and wireframes</li>
    <li>Constraint panel and schedule visualizer</li>
    <li>Scenarios and role-based access</li>
    <li>Testing, deployment and staff training</li>
  </ol>
  <h3 class="st-sub rv">How the design moved in Figma</h3>
  <ol class="st-list st-wide rv">
    <li>Moodboard and ideation</li>
    <li>Audit and UI review</li>
    <li>Colour variations</li>
    <li>Trial-and-error UI rounds</li>
    <li>Versions of the authentication flow</li>
    <li>Presentation for development and final UI feedback</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="st-note rv">Names, emails and figures in the screens below are placeholder data.</p>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s3">
  <p class="st-k rv">03 · Defining the experience</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s3">Requirements from the project brief</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    The project brief set the requirements, and each key design decision answers one of them.
  </p>
  <div class="st-stats st-wide rv">
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">8</p>
      <p>days in the rotating timetable cycle</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">4</p>
      <p>key design decisions, one per requirement</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="st-stat">3</p>
      <p>kinds of user: administrators, teachers and planners</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="st-grid st-g4 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Requirement 01</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">A rule engine</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Hard or soft constraints, with preference weights.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Requirement 02</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Real-time feedback</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Through summary metrics.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Requirement 03</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Saved scenarios</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Timestamped, and ready to reload, compare or continue.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Requirement 04</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Role-based access</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">For administrators, teachers and planners.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s4">
  <p class="st-k rv">04 · Key UX decisions</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s4">Four decisions that shape the tool</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">Each decision answers a requirement from the project brief.</p>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision A</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Put the rules next to the result</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          A Settings panel sits beside the staffing grid. Each constraint has an on/off toggle, a
          weight out of 10 and a Hard Constraint flag, and a set of rules can be saved and switched
          between Current and Saved. Changing a rule and seeing its effect happen in one view.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/keystone-scheduler-constraints.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1324"
        height="828"
        alt="Schedule and Constraint screen: five KPI tiles across the top, a Settings panel of constraints with toggles, weights and Hard Constraint checkboxes on the left, and the staffing grid on the right. Teacher rows are blurred."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>The constraint editor beside the staffing grid. Teacher rows are blurred for privacy.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision B</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Show a schedule’s health at a glance</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          Five tiles sit above the grid: Allocation Success, Teacher Balance Index, Soft Constraint
          Violations, Resource Utilization and Unscheduled Items, with good and bad values in green
          and red. The dashboard adds a ranked overview of every scenario, so an administrator can
          judge a run before reading a single row.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/keystone-scheduler-dashboard.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1400"
        height="905"
        alt="Dashboard with four colour-coded KPI tiles, a Scenario Overview table with progress bars, violation counts and rank badges, and four quick action cards."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>The dashboard: KPI tiles, a ranked scenario overview and quick actions</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision C</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Make experimenting safe</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          Each saved scenario is a card with its saved time, status, version, rank and key metrics.
          Compare stays disabled until two scenarios are selected, and a hint says so. A detail view
          lists the unscheduled items and the version history. Trying an alternative never costs
          earlier work.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="st-figs rv">
      <figure class="cs__fig">
        <img
          src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/keystone-scheduler-scenarios.webp' | rel }}"
          width="1324"
          height="828"
          alt="Scenario Management page with six scenario cards showing saved time, status, version, overall rank and metrics, each with Add to Compare and View Details. The Compare button is disabled, with the hint: Select at least two scenarios to enable comparison."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Scenario cards. Compare stays disabled until two are selected.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="cs__fig">
        <img
          src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/keystone-scheduler-detail.webp' | rel }}"
          width="1514"
          height="946"
          alt="Scenario detail with metrics at the top, a table of unscheduled courses with colour-graded success-rate badges, and a Version History panel on the right with its own Compare action."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Scenario detail: unscheduled items and version history alongside</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="st-split st-wide">
    <div class="st-split__text rv">
      <div class="st-decision">
        <p class="st-k">Decision D</p>
        <h3 class="st-bh">Control who can do what</h3>
        <p class="cs__p">
          A permission matrix sets six modules (Teacher, Course, Venue, Schedule, Scenario and User
          Management) against Add, Edit, View and Delete, with user-group screens for adding users
          and roles. Every permission for a role is readable in a single table.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="cs__fig rv">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/keystone-scheduler-roles.webp' | rel }}"
        width="1400"
        height="875"
        alt="Add Role page with a role title field and a permission matrix of modules against Add, Edit, View and Delete checkboxes."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>Roles and permissions in one matrix</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s5">
  <p class="st-k rv">05 · Visual design direction</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s5">A calm blue shell that saves colour for meaning</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    The header uses a royal blue and actions a brighter blue, while the KPI tiles use blue, green,
    purple and teal.
  </p>
  <div class="st-pal st-wide rv">
    <div class="st-sw st-sw--main st-sw--dark" style="--sw: #2547d0">
      <b>Royal blue</b>
      <span>HEX #2547D0<br />R:37 G:71 B:208</span>
    </div>
    <div class="st-sw st-sw--main st-sw--dark" style="--sw: #335cff">
      <b>Action blue</b>
      <span>HEX #335CFF<br />R:51 G:92 B:255</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="st-grid st-g3 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Colour</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Colour carries meaning</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">Good and bad values use green and red; the shell stays a calm blue.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Scale</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">One grading scale</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">
        Scenarios are graded from Excellent to Poor, on rank badges and success-rate pills alike.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Consistency</p>
      <h3 class="pcard__title">Shared patterns</h3>
      <p class="pcard__text">
        One top navigation, page header and pagination, so each new page feels familiar.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s6">
  <p class="st-k rv">06 · Core screens</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s6">Getting in, setting up and working the grid</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">
    Beyond the four decisions, these screens cover signing in, setting up and working the grid.
    Choose a screen to see it.
  </p>
  <div class="st-wide rv">
    <div class="st-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Core screens">
      <button class="st-tab" type="button" role="tab" id="t-sign" aria-controls="p-sign">Sign-in</button>
      <button class="st-tab" type="button" role="tab" id="t-tea" aria-controls="p-tea">Teachers</button>
      <button class="st-tab" type="button" role="tab" id="t-grid" aria-controls="p-grid">Staffing grid</button>
    </div>
    <div class="st-gal" role="tabpanel" id="p-sign" aria-labelledby="t-sign">
      <p class="st-quote">A single, focused way in</p>
      <p class="cs__p">
        Sign-in is one card with the school crest, and a tilted, blurred timetable shows what is
        behind the login. The full flow also covers verification, a four-step forgot-password path,
        error states, account creation and Microsoft sign-in.
      </p>
      <figure class="cs__fig">
        <img
          src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/keystone-scheduler-signin.webp' | rel }}"
          width="1400"
          height="905"
          alt="Sign-in screen with the Keystone Academy crest, email and password fields and a Sign In button, beside a royal blue panel with a tilted, blurred timetable."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Sign-in, with a glimpse of the timetable behind it</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="st-gal" role="tabpanel" id="p-tea" aria-labelledby="t-tea">
      <p class="st-quote">Setting up the people</p>
      <p class="cs__p">
        A searchable list shows each teacher’s teaching load, home room and grade, with view, edit and
        delete on every row. Schools can add teachers in bulk through Upload CSV.
      </p>
      <figure class="cs__fig">
        <img
          src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/keystone-scheduler-teachers.webp' | rel }}"
          width="1514"
          height="946"
          alt="Teacher Management list with search, Add Teacher, Upload CSV and Export actions, and a table of teachers with email, teaching load, home room and grade."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Teacher management, with bulk upload by CSV</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="st-gal" role="tabpanel" id="p-grid" aria-labelledby="t-grid">
      <p class="st-quote">Keeping a dense grid workable</p>
      <p class="cs__p">
        The staffing grid carries teacher, department, load, preps and per-section counts across
        dozens of columns. Department filter chips, a zoom control, and separate Staffing Grid and
        Schedule tabs let planners narrow the view instead of scrolling through everything.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="cs__sec" aria-labelledby="s7">
  <p class="st-k rv">07 · Goals and next steps</p>
  <h2 class="cs__h rv" id="s7">What the project set out to achieve</h2>
  <p class="cs__p rv">These were the targets in the project brief, not measured results.</p>
  <ol class="st-list st-wide rv">
    <li>Cut scheduling time from weeks to minutes</li>
    <li>Complete an optimization run within 3 minutes on recommended hardware</li>
    <li>Give repeatable results for unchanged inputs</li>
    <li>Let administrators lock assignments and re-optimize the rest</li>
    <li>Keep data on the school’s own infrastructure</li>
  </ol>
  <h3 class="st-sub rv">Planned next steps</h3>
  <div class="st-grid st-g3 st-wide">
    <div class="pcard rv">
      <p class="pcard__word">Next 01</p>
